The paper’s decisive result is Theorem 5, not a generic “random interval constraints” construction: it proves NP-completeness even when the interval graph is exactly two disjoint cliques, by encoding an induced biclique in the signs of one clique. The natural compact witness is therefore a balanced \(k\)-vertex signed subclique (equivalently, its two switching sides), which the checker can validate pairwise without solving the instance. I’m now checking whether a planted distribution of those objects survives the required standard clique/CSP attacks; worst-case NP-completeness alone will not be treated as evidence.
